WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.258
Welcome to Module Seven: Model Optimization.

2
00:00:03.588 --> 00:00:07.335
Here, a trained model becomes smaller, faster, and cheaper to serve.

3
00:00:08.135 --> 00:00:12.309
Picture a team that just fine-tuned an eight-billion-parameter support assistant.

4
00:00:12.639 --> 00:00:14.829
It aces every test, so they launch it.

5
00:00:15.629 --> 00:00:18.929
On launch day, the GPU bill arrives before the praise.

6
00:00:19.259 --> 00:00:24.670
Each replica needs a whole H100, and answers crawl once fifty people chat at once.

7
00:00:25.470 --> 00:00:27.553
The model isn't too dumb; it's too heavy.

8
00:00:27.883 --> 00:00:31.375
Every token streams sixteen gigabytes of weights through memory.

9
00:00:32.175 --> 00:00:35.154
The first fix is quantization: fewer bits per weight.

10
00:00:35.484 --> 00:00:40.042
FP8 halves the bytes and INT4 quarters them, with little accuracy lost.

11
00:00:40.842 --> 00:00:44.312
You'll compute the scales that map real values onto a few levels.

12
00:00:44.642 --> 00:00:49.648
Then you'll compare GPTQ, AWQ, and SmoothQuant, and measure the trade-off.

13
00:00:50.448 --> 00:00:52.489
The second route is fewer parameters.

14
00:00:52.819 --> 00:00:55.563
Structured pruning removes whole layers or channels.

15
00:00:56.363 --> 00:00:59.931
Then the original model teaches the pruned one to recover,

16
00:00:59.981 --> 00:01:02.052
which is NVIDIA's Minitron recipe.

17
00:01:02.382 --> 00:01:05.340
Its students beat same-size models trained from scratch.

18
00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:08.415
Next, you'll look inside the inference engine.

19
00:01:08.745 --> 00:01:11.878
Prefill reads the prompt in one compute-heavy pass,

20
00:01:11.928 --> 00:01:15.373
then decode writes tokens one by one, limited by memory.

21
00:01:16.173 --> 00:01:19.920
Every token leaves keys and values behind in the KV cache.

22
00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:23.379
Its size decides how many users fit on one GPU.

23
00:01:24.179 --> 00:01:28.118
Paged attention stores that cache in small blocks, so little is wasted.

24
00:01:28.448 --> 00:01:32.110
In-flight batching lets new requests join the moment others finish.

25
00:01:32.910 --> 00:01:36.871
Speculative decoding lets a fast draft guess several tokens ahead.

26
00:01:37.201 --> 00:01:41.823
The big model checks them in one pass, so the output is identical, just quicker.

27
00:01:42.623 --> 00:01:47.821
Then you'll build engines with TensorRT-LLM, NVIDIA's fast inference library.

28
00:01:48.151 --> 00:01:51.450
You'll set its cache and batch limits, and serve it behind Triton.

29
00:01:52.250 --> 00:01:55.933
Finally, you'll benchmark with trtllm-bench and tune.

30
00:01:56.263 --> 00:02:00.800
Time to first token, time per output token, and throughput pull against each other.

31
00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:05.689
For our team, FP8 weights and in-flight batching changed everything.

32
00:02:06.019 --> 00:02:09.723
The same model now serves several times the users per GPU.

33
00:02:10.523 --> 00:02:12.251
This is the largest exam domain,

34
00:02:12.301 --> 00:02:15.252
and you'll leave able to make any model lean and fast.

35
00:02:15.582 --> 00:02:19.031
Lesson one is the quantization lab, so let's shrink some weights.
